abstract: >-
  A collection of self-contained research topics that apply artificial
  intelligence methods to cyber-physical and networked systems, each with its
  own research question, code, tests and documentation. The first topic,
  Symbolic AI for Network Restoration, applies three symbolic systems to a
  single domain: the communication backhaul of a smart electric grid. A
  rule-based expert system diagnoses link degradation using 43 production rules
  in five layers, with forward and backward chaining, MYCIN-style certainty
  factors, selectable conflict-resolution policies, and why/how explanation. An
  automated planner represents restoration actions in STRIPS and solves them
  both by GPS means-ends analysis and by A* progression search. An A*
  implementation returns least-cost routes over the network graph, with a
  straight-line heuristic that is admissible and consistent by construction and
  verified exhaustively in tests. The three integrate: a diagnosis becomes the
  planner's initial state, and the reroute action is applicable only when A*
  confirms an alternative route exists. The network topologies are synthetic
  and the diagnostic thresholds are nominal and uncalibrated: no labelled fault
  dataset exists for this domain, which is the stated reason for choosing
  knowledge-driven over data-driven methods. Developed as the practical
  assignments of a doctoral Introduction to Artificial Intelligence course.
